T1	Quotation 6304 6408	"Poor Miss Taylor!--I wish she were here again.  What a pity it is that Mr. Weston ever thought of her!"
T2	Entity 6130 6134	Emma
T3	Entity 6281 6283	he
T4	Cue 6288 6292	said
R1	Speaker Arg1:T3 Arg2:T1	
R2	Cueing Arg1:T4 Arg2:T1	
R3	Addressee Arg1:T2 Arg2:T1	
T5	Quotation 6409 6696	"I cannot agree with you, papa; you know I cannot.  Mr. Weston is such a good-humoured, pleasant, excellent man, that he thoroughly deserves a good wife;-- and you would not have had Miss Taylor live with us for ever, and bear all my odd humours, when she might have a house of her own?"
R4	Speaker Arg1:T2 Arg2:T5	
R5	Addressee Arg1:T3 Arg2:T5	
T6	Quotation 6697 6847	"A house of her own!-- But where is the advantage of a house of her own? This is three times as large.-- And you have never any odd humours, my dear."
R6	Addressee Arg1:T2 Arg2:T6	
R7	Speaker Arg1:T3 Arg2:T6	
T7	Quotation 6848 7006	"How often we shall be going to see them, and they coming to see us!-- We shall be always meeting! We must begin; we must go and pay wedding visit very soon."
R8	Speaker Arg1:T2 Arg2:T7	
R9	Addressee Arg1:T3 Arg2:T7	
T8	Quotation 7007 7101	"My dear, how am I to get so far? Randalls is such a distance.  I could not walk half so far."
R10	Addressee Arg1:T2 Arg2:T8	
R11	Speaker Arg1:T3 Arg2:T8	
T9	Quotation 7102 7186	"No, papa, nobody thought of your walking.  We must go in the carriage, to be sure."
R12	Speaker Arg1:T2 Arg2:T9	
R13	Addressee Arg1:T3 Arg2:T9	
T10	Quotation 7187 7339	"The carriage! But James will not like to put the horses to for such a little way;-- and where are the poor horses to be while we are paying our visit?"
R14	Addressee Arg1:T2 Arg2:T10	
R15	Speaker Arg1:T3 Arg2:T10	
T11	Quotation 7340 7813	"They are to be put into Mr. Weston's stable, papa.  You know we have settled all that already.  We talked it all over with Mr. Weston last night.  And as for James, you may be very sure he will always like going to Randalls, because of his daughter's being housemaid there.  I only doubt whether he will ever take us anywhere else.  That was your doing, papa.  You got Hannah that good place.  Nobody thought of Hannah till you mentioned her-- James is so obliged to you!"
R16	Speaker Arg1:T2 Arg2:T11	
R17	Speaker Arg1:T3 Arg2:T11	
T12	Quotation 7814 8559	"I am very glad I did think of her.  It was very lucky, for I would not have had poor James think himself slighted upon any account; and I am sure she will make a very good servant: she is a civil, pretty-spoken girl; I have a great opinion of her.  Whenever I see her, she always curtseys and asks me how I do, in a very pretty manner; and when you have had her here to do needlework, I observe she always turns the lock of the door the right way and never bangs it.  I am sure she will be an excellent servant; and it will be a great comfort to poor Miss Taylor to have somebody about her that she is used to see.  Whenever James goes over to see his daughter, you know, she will be hearing of us.  He will be able to tell her how we all are."
R18	Addressee Arg1:T2 Arg2:T12	
R19	Speaker Arg1:T3 Arg2:T12	
T13	Entity 9640 9653	Mr. Woodhouse
T14	Cue 9665 9673	observed
T15	Quotation 9675 9810	"It is very kind of you, Mr. Knightley, to come out at this late hour to call upon us.  I am afraid you must have had a shocking walk."
R20	Cueing Arg1:T14 Arg2:T15	
R21	Speaker Arg1:T13 Arg2:T15	
T16	Entity 9458 9471	Mr. Knightley
R22	Addressee Arg1:T16 Arg2:T15	
T17	Quotation 9811 9921	"Not at all, sir.  It is a beautiful moonlight night; and so mild that I must draw back from your great fire."
R23	Addressee Arg1:T13 Arg2:T17	
R24	Speaker Arg1:T16 Arg2:T17	
T18	Quotation 9922 10003	"But you must have found it very damp and dirty.  I wish you may not catch cold."
R25	Speaker Arg1:T13 Arg2:T18	
R26	Addressee Arg1:T16 Arg2:T18	
T19	Quotation 10004 10057	"Dirty, sir! Look at my shoes.  Not a speck on them."
R27	Speaker Arg1:T16 Arg2:T19	
R28	Addressee Arg1:T13 Arg2:T19	
T20	Quotation 10058 10244	"Well! that is quite surprising, for we have had a vast deal of rain here.  It rained dreadfully hard for half an hour while we were at breakfast.  I wanted them to put off the wedding."
R29	Speaker Arg1:T13 Arg2:T20	
R30	Addressee Arg1:T16 Arg2:T20	
T21	Quotation 10245 10489	"By the bye--I have not wished you joy.  Being pretty well aware of what sort of joy you must both be feeling, I have been in no hurry with my congratulations; but I hope it all went off tolerably well.  How did you all behave? Who cried most?"
R31	Speaker Arg1:T16 Arg2:T21	
R32	Addressee Arg1:T13 Arg2:T21	
T22	Quotation 10490 10534	"Ah! poor Miss Taylor! 'Tis a sad business."
R33	Addressee Arg1:T16 Arg2:T22	
R34	Speaker Arg1:T13 Arg2:T22	
T23	Quotation 10535 10801	"Poor Mr. and Miss Woodhouse, if you please; but I cannot possibly say 'poor Miss Taylor.' I have a great regard for you and Emma; but when it comes to the question of dependence or independence!-- At any rate, it must be better to have only one to please than two."
R35	Speaker Arg1:T16 Arg2:T23	
R36	Addressee Arg1:T13 Arg2:T23	
T24	Quotation 10802 10878	"Especially when one of those two is such a fanciful, troublesome creature!"
T25	Cue 10879 10883	said
T26	Entity 10884 10888	Emma
R37	Cueing Arg1:T25 Arg2:T24	
R38	Speaker Arg1:T26 Arg2:T24	
R39	Addressee Arg1:T16 Arg2:T24	
T27	Quotation 10901 11006	"That is what you have in your head, I know-- and what you would certainly say if my father were not by."
R40	Addressee Arg1:T26 Arg2:T27	
R41	Speaker Arg1:T16 Arg2:T27	
T28	Quotation 11007 11052	"I believe it is very true, my dear, indeed,"
T29	Cue 11053 11057	said
T30	Entity 11058 11071	Mr. Woodhouse
R42	Cueing Arg1:T29 Arg2:T28	
R43	Speaker Arg1:T30 Arg2:T28	
R44	Addressee Arg1:T26 Arg2:T28	
T31	Quotation 11087 11146	"I am afraid I am sometimes very fanciful and troublesome."
R45	Cueing Arg1:T29 Arg2:T31	
R46	Speaker Arg1:T30 Arg2:T31	
R47	Addressee Arg1:T26 Arg2:T31	
T32	Quotation 11147 11418	"My dearest papa! You do not think I could mean you, or suppose Mr. Knightley to mean you.  What a horrible idea! Oh no! I meant only myself.  Mr. Knightley loves to find fault with me, you know-- in a joke-- it is all a joke.  We always say what we like to one another."
R48	Speaker Arg1:T26 Arg2:T32	
R49	Addressee Arg1:T30 Arg2:T32	
T33	Quotation 11780 11813	"Emma knows I never flatter her,"
T34	Cue 11814 11818	said
T35	Entity 11819 11832	Mr. Knightley
T36	Quotation 11834 12003	"but I meant no reflection on any body.  Miss Taylor has been used to have two persons to please; she will now have but one.  The chances are that she must be a gainer."
R50	Cueing Arg1:T34 Arg2:T33	
R51	Cueing Arg1:T34 Arg2:T36	
R52	Speaker Arg1:T35 Arg2:T33	
R53	Speaker Arg1:T35 Arg2:T36	
R54	Addressee Arg1:T30 Arg2:T33	
R55	Addressee Arg1:T30 Arg2:T36	
T37	Quotation 12004 12011	"Well,"
T38	Cue 12012 12016	said
T39	Entity 12017 12021	Emma
T40	Quotation 12048 12360	"you want to hear about the wedding; and I shall be happy to tell you, for we all behaved charmingly.  Every body was punctual, every body in their best looks: not a tear, and hardly a long face to be seen.  Oh no; we all felt that we were going to be only half a mile apart, and were sure of meeting every day."
R56	Cueing Arg1:T38 Arg2:T37	
R57	Speaker Arg1:T39 Arg2:T37	
R58	Cueing Arg1:T38 Arg2:T40	
R59	Speaker Arg1:T39 Arg2:T40	
R60	Addressee Arg1:T35 Arg2:T37	
R61	Addressee Arg1:T35 Arg2:T40	
T41	Quotation 12361 12399	"Dear Emma bears every thing so well,"
T42	Cue 12400 12404	said
T43	Entity 12405 12415	her father
R62	Cueing Arg1:T42 Arg2:T41	
R63	Speaker Arg1:T43 Arg2:T41	
R64	Addressee Arg1:T35 Arg2:T41	
T44	Quotation 12418 12548	"But, Mr. Knightley, she is really very sorry to lose poor Miss Taylor, and I am sure she will miss her more than she thinks for."
R65	Cueing Arg1:T42 Arg2:T44	
R66	Speaker Arg1:T43 Arg2:T44	
R67	Addressee Arg1:T35 Arg2:T44	
T45	Quotation 12611 12673	"It is impossible that Emma should not miss such a companion,"
T46	Cue 12674 12678	said
T47	Entity 12679 12692	Mr. Knightley
R68	Cueing Arg1:T46 Arg2:T45	
R69	Speaker Arg1:T47 Arg2:T45	
R70	Addressee Arg1:T43 Arg2:T45	
T48	Quotation 12695 13151	"We should not like her so well as we do, sir, if we could suppose it; but she knows how much the marriage is to Miss Taylor's advantage; she knows how very acceptable it must be, at Miss Taylor's time of life, to be settled in a home of her own, and how important to her to be secure of a comfortable provision, and therefore cannot allow herself to feel so much pain as pleasure.  Every friend of Miss Taylor must be glad to have her so happily married."
R71	Cueing Arg1:T46 Arg2:T48	
R72	Speaker Arg1:T47 Arg2:T48	
R73	Addressee Arg1:T43 Arg2:T48	
T49	Quotation 13152 13201	"And you have forgotten one matter of joy to me,"
T50	Cue 13202 13206	said
T51	Entity 13207 13211	Emma
T52	Quotation 13213 13465	"and a very considerable one-- that I made the match myself.  I made the match, you know, four years ago; and to have it take place, and be proved in the right, when so many people said Mr. Weston would never marry again, may comfort me for any thing."
R74	Cueing Arg1:T50 Arg2:T49	
R75	Cueing Arg1:T50 Arg2:T52	
R76	Speaker Arg1:T51 Arg2:T49	
R77	Speaker Arg1:T51 Arg2:T52	
T53	Entity 13466 13479	Mr. Knightley
R78	Addressee Arg1:T53 Arg2:T49	
R79	Addressee Arg1:T53 Arg2:T52	
T54	Quotation 13531 13679	"Ah! my dear, I wish you would not make matches and foretell things, for whatever you say always comes to pass.  Pray do not make any more matches."
T55	Entity 13504 13514	Her father
T56	Cue 13522 13529	replied
R80	Cueing Arg1:T56 Arg2:T54	
R81	Speaker Arg1:T55 Arg2:T54	
T57	Entity 13504 13507	Her
R82	Addressee Arg1:T57 Arg2:T54	
T58	Quotation 13680 14915	"I promise you to make none for myself, papa; but I must, indeed, for other people.  It is the greatest amusement in the world! And after such success, you know!-- Every body said that Mr. Weston would never marry again.  Oh dear, no! Mr. Weston, who had been a widower so long, and who seemed so perfectly comfortable without a wife, so constantly occupied either in his business in town or among his friends here, always acceptable wherever he went, always cheerful-- Mr. Weston need not spend a single evening in the year alone if he did not like it.  Oh no! Mr. Weston certainly would never marry again.  Some people even talked of a promise to his wife on her deathbed, and others of the son and the uncle not letting him.  All manner of solemn nonsense was talked on the subject, but I believed none of it.  Ever since the day-- about four years ago-- that Miss Taylor and I met with him in Broadway Lane, when, because it began to drizzle, he darted away with so much gallantry, and borrowed two umbrellas for us from Farmer Mitchell's, I made up my mind on the subject.  I planned the match from that hour; and when such success has blessed me in this instance, dear papa, you cannot think that I shall leave off match-making."
R83	Speaker Arg1:T57 Arg2:T58	
R84	Addressee Arg1:T55 Arg2:T58	
T59	Quotation 14916 14965	"I do not understand what you mean by 'success,'"
T60	Cue 14966 14970	said
T61	Entity 14971 14984	Mr. Knightley
R85	Cueing Arg1:T60 Arg2:T59	
R86	Speaker Arg1:T61 Arg2:T59	
T62	Quotation 14986 15616	"Success supposes endeavour.  Your time has been properly and delicately spent, if you have been endeavouring for the last four years to bring about this marriage.  A worthy employment for a young lady's mind! But if, which I rather imagine, your making the match, as you call it, means only your planning it, your saying to yourself one idle day, 'I think it would be a very good thing for Miss Taylor if Mr. Weston were to marry her,' and saying it again to yourself every now and then afterwards, why do you talk of success? Where is your merit? What are you proud of? You made a lucky guess; and that is all that can be said."
R87	Cueing Arg1:T60 Arg2:T62	
R88	Speaker Arg1:T61 Arg2:T62	
R89	Addressee Arg1:T57 Arg2:T62	
T63	Quotation 15617 16287	"And have you never known the pleasure and triumph of a lucky guess?-- I pity you.--I thought you cleverer-- for, depend upon it a lucky guess is never merely luck.  There is always some talent in it.  And as to my poor word 'success,' which you quarrel with, I do not know that I am so entirely without any claim to it.  You have drawn two pretty pictures; but I think there may be a third--a something between the do-nothing and the do-all. If I had not promoted Mr. Weston's visits here, and given many little encouragements, and smoothed many little matters, it might not have come to any thing after all.  I think you must know Hartfield enough to comprehend that."
R90	Addressee Arg1:T61 Arg2:T63	
R91	Speaker Arg1:T57 Arg2:T63	
T64	Quotation 16288 16526	"A straightforward, open-hearted man like Weston, and a rational, unaffected woman like Miss Taylor, may be safely left to manage their own concerns.  You are more likely to have done harm to yourself, than good to them, by interference."
R92	Speaker Arg1:T61 Arg2:T64	
R93	Addressee Arg1:T57 Arg2:T64	
T65	Quotation 16527 16588	"Emma never thinks of herself, if she can do good to others,"
T66	Cue 16589 16597	rejoined
T67	Entity 16598 16611	Mr. Woodhouse
R94	Cueing Arg1:T66 Arg2:T65	
R95	Speaker Arg1:T67 Arg2:T65	
T68	Quotation 16641 16759	"But, my dear, pray do not make any more matches; they are silly things, and break up one's family circle grievously."
R96	Cueing Arg1:T66 Arg2:T68	
R97	Speaker Arg1:T67 Arg2:T68	
R98	Addressee Arg1:T61 Arg2:T65	
R99	Addressee Arg1:T57 Arg2:T68	
T69	Quotation 16760 17296	"Only one more, papa; only for Mr. Elton.  Poor Mr. Elton! You like Mr. Elton, papa,--I must look about for a wife for him.  There is nobody in Highbury who deserves him-- and he has been here a whole year, and has fitted up his house so comfortably, that it would be a shame to have him single any longer-- and I thought when he was joining their hands to-day, he looked so very much as if he would like to have the same kind office done for him! I think very well of Mr. Elton, and this is the only way I have of doing him a service."
R100	Addressee Arg1:T67 Arg2:T69	
R101	Speaker Arg1:T57 Arg2:T69	
T70	Quotation 17297 17598	"Mr. Elton is a very pretty young man, to be sure, and a very good young man, and I have a great regard for him.  But if you want to shew him any attention, my dear, ask him to come and dine with us some day. That will be a much better thing.  I dare say Mr. Knightley will be so kind as to meet him."
R102	Speaker Arg1:T67 Arg2:T70	
R103	Addressee Arg1:T61 Arg2:T70	
R104	Addressee Arg1:T57 Arg2:T70	
T71	Quotation 17599 17649	"With a great deal of pleasure, sir, at any time,"
T72	Cue 17650 17654	said
T73	Entity 17655 17668	Mr. Knightley
T74	Quotation 17680 17944	"and I agree with you entirely, that it will be a much better thing.  Invite him to dinner, Emma, and help him to the best of the fish and the chicken, but leave him to chuse his own wife.  Depend upon it, a man of six or seven-and-twenty can take care of himself.
